            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <windows>

The windows installed in this house 30 years ago were top quality and had a low E coating with argon gas fill between the 2 panes of glass. There are some units where the seal between the panes of glass has deteriorated and some moisture has created a slight "fog" in the upper part of the windows. There is a larger problem though in that this house has inadequate ventilation and the upper floor windows have suffered from deterioration due to excess condensation, frost &amp; mould buildup along the lower edges.



I mentioned that although new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows will be more energy efficient, the lack of adequate ventilation in the house will likely still allow for condensation &amp; perhaps even frost to form .......... especially if upper bedroom doors are closed a lot or if window blinds are not opened during the day.



You had mentioned that you had a quote for replacing all the windows in the house. I modeled the estimated energy savings associated with upgrading all of the windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows (except for the skylights), with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 293 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 11.8 GJ.



There are currently no rebates for upgrading windows, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), which requires an eligible wall insulation &amp; siding upgrade to the house. We talked about that, and your house walls already have interior strapping (with additional insulation) so there is very little value to adding even more wall insulation on the exterior of the walls.

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. See the Additional Comments section of this report where I talk about this further, including my comments on the "Passive Ventilation" system currently installed in your house.



Your I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. I recommend you install one that is a minimum 75% efficient and has an ECM circulation motor to keep operation costs low. Perhaps a system designer can find a creative way to integrate this HRV into the existing bedroom supply pipe network that is currently in place ?



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase the ventilation of your house (which is required), with an estimated $ 83 increase per year in energy costs, increasing your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 182 litre external storage buffer tank. The boiler appears to be original to when the house was built.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 6" chimney or the 6" combustion air supply pipe.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,572 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 59 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, 3 bathroom fans, 1 laundry room fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 9.0 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent - your current oil boiler is chimney vented &amp; the combustion air supply for it has a cover blocking the air flow.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector? Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:



        - Kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 bathroom fans

        - 1 exhaust fan in the laundry room

        - A clothes dryer

 

 **** WITH, a passive makeup fresh air supply (that big rubbermaid container in the basement with all the pipes connected). This unit had an intentional design to allow air from outside to be drawn into the house each time there was air being exhausted in a bathroom, kitchen or laundry area (or even if a window upstairs is partially open). The problem is this design is the fresh air does not always go to the rooms that require it the most (upstairs bedrooms) or in the quantity of fresh air that may be required in order to prevent poor indoor air quality or excessive condensation buildup on windows. I also noticed in your house that, once the kids all get home from school, the bedroom doors are all being shut for privacy - this further restricts heat &amp; ventilation for these bedrooms. Unfortunately, many of the original wood windows in your house are suffering damage from many years of inadequate ventilation and condensation/frost/mould buildup along the lower sill plates.



Overall, your house is currently too air tight and would benefit from additional ventilation - please see the section of this report that mentions the installation of a Heat Recovery Ventilation system.



Additionally, you may want to consider installing timer switches on the 3 bathroom exhaust fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they can be run a little longer to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
